"""Check that the wasm32 clippy step refuses what it says it refuses, and that
the wasm32 AES-GCM stack is built with its zeroize features.

The clippy step (`devtools/run-wasm-step clippy`) lints the workspace crates
the adapter's wasm32 build compiles, in the release profile, with the method
and type lists of devtools/wasm-clippy/clippy.toml and clippy's
await_holding_lock and await_holding_refcell_ref lints. Its rule: every call
to a listed method or use of a listed type that the step lints sits under an
accepted allow, and the two await lints report no std lock guard or RefCell
borrow held across an .await. An accepted allow is an outer `#[allow(..)]`
or `#[expect(..)]` that names clippy::disallowed_methods or
clippy::disallowed_types in that spelling and has a `reason` holding more
than whitespace, on a `fn` item, or on a statement after a `{`, `;` or `}`:
a `let`, or an expression ending in `;` that is not a macro invocation and
does not start with a block. It covers the code written in that function or
statement, every call there, but not the items written inside it: each of
those (a nested function, an impl and its methods) needs its own allow. One
on a `mod mNNNN_<name>;` item with no path attribute, in the migrations/mod.rs
in the directory of a crate's library root, which declares a code-body
migration (CONTRIBUTING.md), is accepted too and covers the module's file,
mNNNN_<name>.rs beside it, whole: a checksum test pins a released body, so an
allow cannot go inside it. The guard checks that form only; whether the
module is a released body is for review. Of the reason, the guard checks
only that it is there; whether it says why the wasm32 build never runs the
calls the allow covers is for review.

The script copies the working tree (the files git tracks or does not ignore,
uncommitted edits included), plants probes and controls in the copy, and runs
the clippy step there twice: as it is (W), and with --force-warn of the four
lints (F), which reports each of them, as a warning, whatever lint setting
would switch it off or lower it, in each crate clippy lints. It fails, with
one line per problem, unless:
1. each report of F, but those of the probes, is a disallowed call or type
   that an accepted allow covers as written (where the report points) and
   that W does not report; and F reports no await-holding lint;
2. F reports the probe of each clippy.toml entry; clippy.toml holds only the
   two lists, each entry has a probe and each probe an entry, and neither run
   prints a warning about clippy.toml (an entry that does not resolve is only
   a warning, which -D warnings does not raise);
3. no allow or expect written in a file the build reads, in a cfg_attr too,
   names one of the two lints in that spelling unless it is accepted (an
   allow in another form, such as a lint group, a raw identifier or one a
   macro builds, is not reported, and item 1 refuses the calls under it);
4. both runs finish (of a run that does not, the guard reports the errors
   and the crates it did not finish linting, and judges nothing else), and
   in each, every crate's dep-info records the CLIPPY_ARGS and
   CLIPPY_CONF_DIR the step passed (a build script's cargo::rustc-env can
   change either for its crate, a forced [env] entry for every crate); the
   environment sets none of RUSTC, RUSTC_WRAPPER, RUSTC_WORKSPACE_WRAPPER and
   their CARGO_BUILD_ forms, and no cargo configuration file the copy's runs
   read sets build.rustc, build.rustc-wrapper or
   build.rustc-workspace-wrapper (another program as the compiler can change
   its options for one crate);
5. in the adapter's wasm32 build as `cargo tree -e normal` resolves it, each
   of aes, aes-gcm, ghash and polyval is there as one copy (one package: one
   name, version and source), with its zeroize feature on;
6. the guard can plant every probe and control (_plant and plant_controls
   say when it cannot); each of BUILD_SCRIPT and MIGRATIONS_CONTROLS goes
   into the first crate of the build, by name, that can take it
   (_own_crate_plants). When a run does not finish, the plants that could
   not be made are reported before its errors.
Each check runs, in the same call, on the tree and on controls planted for
it, but for two: the plant check, which no control makes fail, and the check
that both runs finish, which a control could test only by stopping the
guard. The controls: CONTROLS, OTHER_FILE_CONTROLS, BUILD_SCRIPT,
RELEASE_CONTROL, MIGRATIONS_CONTROLS, CONTROL_PROBE, CONTROL_ENTRY,
CONTROL_ENVIRONMENT, CONTROL_CARGO_CONFIG and two zeroize graphs; and each
run's dep-info must be read for every crate of the build. A control that does not give the finding it was planted for, and
a finding no control expects, fail the guard. The environment and
cargo-configuration checks are controlled on their control inputs only:
handed an empty mapping or file list in place of the tree's, the guard would
still pass. A wrapper that caps one crate's lints is caught all the same.
rustc reports a force-warned lint under --cap-lints as well, so F reports
that crate's calls, and item 1 refuses those no accepted allow covers.
clippy-driver, though, lints no crate whose own compiler options hold
--cap-lints allow and no --force-warn of a clippy lint (the step's options,
which reach it in CLIPPY_ARGS, do not count): that crate's dep-info then
records no CLIPPY_CONF_DIR, which item 4 refuses.

What it does not see is listed below. The headless parity corpus
(`devtools/run-wasm-step corpus`), which runs the module under Node, finds a
trap in such code only on a path one of its cases takes.
- code no clippy run compiles: code under a cfg that is false when clippy
  runs, such as cfg(not(clippy)) (clippy-driver sets cfg(clippy)), or under
  a cfg a build script sets from what it sees (it sees the CLIPPY_ARGS that
  cargo clippy sets); code compiled only in another profile, such as a
  cfg(debug_assertions) branch, which a dev build compiles and a release
  build does not; and code compiled only under a feature the adapter's
  wasm32 build does not enable;
- calls a dependency makes in its own code, which clippy does not lint: moka
  calls std::time::Instant::now() when a cache is built
  (src/common/time/clock.rs), so building an extenddb-cache SwrCache with
  SwrCache::try_new or SwrCache::new traps on wasm32 while the step passes
  (only the server builds one today);
- calls clippy.toml does not list;
- who calls the code an accepted allow covers: the allow covers the calls
  written there, whatever reaches them, so a new caller of an allowed
  function, on a path a wasm32 request takes, passes the step and the guard
  while the allow's reason no longer holds;
- a std lock guard or a RefCell borrow held inside another value (an Option,
  a struct, a tuple) across an .await, which the two await lints do not
  report;
- a build script or a procedural macro that behaves differently in the copy
  (its path differs).
A call that a macro defined elsewhere expands to is reported where the macro
writes it, so an allow at the invocation does not cover it. A macro can build
an item from tokens written under an accepted allow, which then covers that
item; inside a function, rustc's non_local_definitions lint, an error under the
step's -D warnings, reports such an impl of a type defined outside it.

The runs use the caller's cargo target directory; nothing else of the
caller's working tree is written. Every command the guard starts runs with
CARGO_TERM_COLOR=always, and each cargo command whose output it reads with
--color never. The copy is removed on exit, also on
SIGINT, SIGTERM and SIGHUP, and a clippy run is stopped with it. Needs Python
3.11 or later (for tomllib) and git; exit status 0 means every check passed.
"""

